---
import IllustrationFrame from './IllustrationFrame.astro';
import { riskIllustration, SIGNALS } from '../lib/artifacts/risk-signals.mjs';

const r = riskIllustration();
---
<IllustrationFrame
  title="What changes when three warning lists become one ranking."
  purpose="It combines three invented signal lists for invented market participants into one score."
>
  <table class="decision-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>List (each owned by a different team)</th><th>Top {r.topN} participants</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {SIGNALS.map((s) => (
        <tr><td>{s.label}</td><td>{r.perList[s.key].join(', ')}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Across the three lists, {r.distinctOnLists} different participants appear, and only {r.onMoreThanOne} appear on more
    than one. Each team sees its own slice.
  </p>
  <table class="decision-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Combined rank</th><th>Participant</th><th>Score (0 to 1)</th><th>Top-{r.topN} lists it is on</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {r.integrated.map((p, i) => (
        <tr><td>{i + 1}</td><td>{p.name}</td><td>{p.score.toFixed(2)}</td><td>{p.lists}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    {r.surfacedByCombining.length} of the combined top {r.topN} ({r.surfacedByCombining.join(', ')}) are on at most one
    single list. A participant can be a little worrying on every list and still never reach the top of any of them.
  </p>
  <p>
    This does not reproduce the framework, its metrics or the 32% figure above. It only shows why a combined view finds
    cases that separate lists miss.
  </p>
  <Fragment slot="method">
    <p>
      {r.participants} invented participants. Counts per list are random and independent. Within each list a participant
      is ranked, ranks are scaled to 0 to 1 (ties share a rank), and the three scaled ranks are added with weights
      {SIGNALS.map((s) => ` ${s.label.toLowerCase()} ${s.weight}`).join(',')}. The weights are invented. Code:
      <code>src/lib/artifacts/risk-signals.mjs</code>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</IllustrationFrame>
